      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the number of odd numbers between 100 and 999 that have distinct digits, we need to consider the structure of the number. An odd number must end in an odd digit, so the possible last digits are 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9. We will consider each case separately.\n\n**Case 1: The last digit is 1.**\nThe first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 except 1 (to ensure distinct digits), so there are 8 possible choices for the first digit. The second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 except the first digit and 1, so there are 8 possible choices for the second digit. Therefore, the number of such numbers is:\n\\[ 8 \\times 8 = 64 \\]\n\n**Case 2: The last digit is 3.**\nSimilarly, the first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 except 3 and the last digit (3), so there are 7 possible choices for the first digit. The second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 except the first digit and 3, so there are 8 possible choices for the second digit. Therefore, the number of such numbers is:\n\\[ 7 \\times 8 = 56 \\]\n\n**Case 3: The last digit is 5.**\nSimilarly, the first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 except 5 and the last digit (5), so there are 7 possible choices for the first digit. The second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 except the first digit and 5, so there are 8 possible choices for the second digit. Therefore, the number of such numbers is:\n\\[ 7 \\times 8 = 56 \\]\n\n**Case 4: The last digit is 7.**\nSimilarly, the first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 except 7 and the last digit (7), so there are 7 possible choices for the first digit. The second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 except the first digit and 7, so there are 8 possible choices for the second digit. Therefore, the number of such numbers is:\n\\[ 7 \\times 8 = 56 \\]\n\n**Case 5: The last digit is 9.**\nSimilarly, the first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 except 9 and the last digit (9), so there are 7 possible choices for the first digit. The second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 except the first digit and 9, so there are 8 possible choices for the second digit. Therefore, the number of such numbers is:\n\\[ 7 \\times 8 = 56 \\]\n\nAdding the number of possible numbers from all cases, we get:\n\\[ 64 + 56 + 56 + 56 + 56 = 292 \\]\n\nThus, the number of odd numbers between 100 and 999 that have distinct digits is:\n\\[ \\boxed{292} \\]",
